//! Composable external iteration.
//!
//! If you have a collection of some kind and need to perform an operation on
//! its elements, you will quickly run into iterators. Iterators are used
//! heavily in idiomatic navi code, as in Rust, so it is worth becoming
//! familiar with them.
//!
//! # Organization
//!
//! This module is largely organized by type:
//!
//! * Traits are the core portion: [`Iterator`] says what an iterator is,
//!   [`IntoIterator`] what can become one (and so what a `for` loop takes),
//!   [`FromIterator`] and [`Extend`] what can be built or extended from one,
//!   [`DoubleEndedIterator`] and [`ExactSizeIterator`] what some iterators can
//!   do in addition.
//! * Functions such as [`once`], [`repeat`] and [`empty`] make iterators.
//! * Structs are the types of the iterators the adapters of [`Iterator`]
//!   return, such as [`Map`] (returned by [`Iterator::map`]).
//!
//! # Iterator
//!
//! The heart of this module is the [`Iterator`] trait:
//!
//! ```text
//! pub trait Iterator {
//!     type Item;
//!     fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! An iterator has a method, `next`, which when called returns an
//! [`Option`]`<Self::Item>`: `Some(item)` as long as there are elements,
//! and [`None`] once it is exhausted. All the other methods of [`Iterator`]
//! have default implementations built on `next`.
//!
//! Iterators are also composable: the adapters chain them together to do
//! more complex forms of processing.
//!
//! # The three forms of iteration
//!
//! There are three common methods which create iterators from a collection:
//!
//! * `iter()`, which iterates over `&T`.
//! * `iter_mut()`, which iterates over `&mut T`.
//! * `into_iter()`, which iterates over `T`.
//!
//! navi has no moves: `into_iter()` does not use up the collection. A
//! collection such as [`Vec`] is a handle to a shared container, so its
//! `into_iter()` iterates over that container, and the vector stays usable.
//!
//! ```
//! let v = Vec::from([1, 2, 3]);
//! let mut sum = 0;
//! for x in v.into_iter() {
//!     sum += x;
//! }
//! assert_eq(sum, 6);
//! assert_eq(v.len(), 3);
//! ```
//!
//! # Implementing Iterator
//!
//! Creating an iterator of your own involves two steps: creating a struct to
//! hold the iterator's state, and then implementing [`Iterator`] for it.
//!
//! ```
//! // An iterator which counts from one to five.
//! struct Counter {
//!     count: usize,
//! }
//!
//! impl Iterator for Counter {
//!     type Item = usize;
//!
//!     fn next(&mut self) -> Option<usize> {
//!         if self.count < 5 {
//!             self.count += 1;
//!             Some(self.count)
//!         } else {
//!             None
//!         }
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! let mut counter = Counter { count: 0 };
//! assert_eq(counter.next(), Some(1));
//! assert_eq(counter.next(), Some(2));
//! assert_eq(counter.next(), Some(3));
//! assert_eq(counter.next(), Some(4));
//! assert_eq(counter.next(), Some(5));
//! assert_eq(counter.next(), None);
//!
//! // All of Iterator's other methods come for free:
//! let sum: usize = Counter { count: 0 }.filter(|x| *x % 2 == 1).sum();
//! assert_eq(sum, 9);
//! ```
//!
//! # for loops and IntoIterator
//!
//! A `for` loop calls [`IntoIterator::into_iter`] on the value it is given,
//! and then `next` until it returns [`None`]. Every [`Iterator`] implements
//! [`IntoIterator`] by returning itself, so a `for` loop takes both
//! collections and iterators:
//!
//! ```
//! let values = Vec::from([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]);
//! let mut seen = Vec::new();
//! for x in values.iter().map(|x| *x * 10) {
//!     seen.push(x);
//! }
//! assert_eq(seen, Vec::from([10, 20, 30, 40, 50]));
//! ```
//!
//! # Adapters and laziness
//!
//! Functions which take an iterator and return another iterator, such as
//! [`Iterator::map`], [`Iterator::filter`] or [`Iterator::take`], are called
//! iterator adapters. They are lazy: creating one does nothing until it is
//! consumed, for example by a `for` loop or by [`Iterator::collect`],
//! [`Iterator::fold`] or [`Iterator::for_each`].
//!
//! ```
//! let v = Vec::from([1, 2, 3]);
//! let mut calls = 0;
//! let it = v.iter().inspect(|_| calls += 1);
//! assert_eq(calls, 0); // nothing has run yet
//! assert_eq(it.count(), 3);
//! assert_eq(calls, 3);
//! ```
//!
//! # Infinity
//!
//! Iterators do not have to be finite: the range `0..` and [`repeat`] go on
//! forever. Adapters such as [`Iterator::take`] make a finite iterator from
//! an infinite one:
//!
//! ```
//! let numbers: Vec<i32> = (0..).map(|x| x * x).take(4).collect();
//! assert_eq(numbers, Vec::from([0, 1, 4, 9]));
//! ```
//!
//! Consuming an infinite iterator with a method like [`Iterator::count`] or
//! [`Iterator::max`] never returns.
//!
//! # Differences from Rust
//!
//! Closure parameters are fn types: Rust's `F: FnMut(Self::Item) -> B` is
//! `fn(Self::Item) -> B` here (see spec/std-diff.md). A closure taking a
//! reference sometimes needs its parameter type written, as in
//! `|x: &&i32| -> bool { **x > 1 }`. Also:
//!
//! * [`Iterator::cloned`], [`Iterator::copied`] and [`Iterator::flatten`]
//!   return [`Cloned<I, T>`](Cloned), [`Copied<I, T>`](Copied) and [`Flatten<I,
//!   U>`](Flatten), with the type of the items (or of what they refer to) as a
//!   second type parameter.
//! * There is no `Iterator::eq`, `ne`, `lt`, `le`, `gt`, `ge`, `cmp` or
//!   `partial_cmp` (they would be chosen over [`PartialEq`] and [`Ord`]'s
//!   methods of the same names), and no `try_fold` or `try_for_each` (there is
//!   no `Try` trait).
//! * [`Flatten`], [`FlatMap`] and [`StepBy`] are not double-ended.

/// An interface for dealing with iterators.
///
/// This is the main iterator trait. An iterator has one required method,
/// [`Self::next`], which yields the elements one at a time, wrapped in
/// [`Some`], and then [`None`] once there are no more. All the other methods
/// have default implementations built on `next`. For more about the concept
/// of iterators generally, see the module-level documentation of
/// `std::iter`.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// struct Countdown {
///     n: u32,
/// }
///
/// impl Iterator for Countdown {
///     type Item = u32;
///
///     fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {
///         if self.n == 0 {
///             None
///         } else {
///             self.n -= 1;
///             Some(self.n + 1)
///         }
///     }
/// }
///
/// let v: Vec<u32> = Countdown { n: 3 }.collect();
/// assert_eq(v, Vec::from([3u32, 2, 1]));
/// ```
pub trait Iterator {
    /// The type of the elements being iterated over.
    type Item;
    /// Advances the iterator and returns the next value.
    ///
    /// Returns [`None`] when iteration is finished. Individual iterator
    /// implementations may choose to resume iteration, and so calling `next()`
    /// again may or may not eventually start returning `Some(item)` again at
    /// some point; [`Self::fuse`] makes an iterator that does not.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// // A call to next() returns the next value...
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    ///
    /// // ... and then None once it's over.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    ///
    /// // More calls may or may not return None. Here, they always will.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;

    /// Returns the bounds on the remaining length of the iterator.
    ///
    /// Specifically, `size_hint()` returns a tuple where the first element is
    /// the lower bound, and the second element is the upper bound. The upper
    /// bound is an [`Option`]: [`None`] means either that there is no known
    /// upper bound, or that it is larger than `usize`.
    ///
    /// The default implementation returns `(0, None)`, which is correct for any
    /// iterator. An implementation that yields fewer or more elements than its
    /// hint is buggy, but nothing may rely on the hint for correctness: it is
    /// meant for optimizations such as reserving space for the elements.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    /// assert_eq(iter.size_hint(), (3, Some(3)));
    /// let _ = iter.next();
    /// assert_eq(iter.size_hint(), (2, Some(2)));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// [`Self::filter`] cannot know how many elements will pass, only that no
    /// more than all of them will:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
    /// let iter = a.iter().filter(|x| **x % 2 == 0);
    /// assert_eq(iter.size_hint(), (0, Some(4)));
    /// ```
    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        (0, None)
    }

    /// Consumes the iterator, counting the number of iterations and returning
    /// it.
    ///
    /// This method calls [`Self::next`] repeatedly until [`None`] is
    /// encountered, returning the number of times it saw [`Some`]. Note that
    /// `next` has to be called at least once even if the iterator does not have
    /// any elements.
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// Panics if the iterator has more than `usize::MAX` elements (the count
    /// overflows).
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().count(), 3);
    ///
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().count(), 5);
    /// ```
    fn count(self) -> usize {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut n = 0;
        while let Some(_) = it.next() {
            n += 1;
        }
        n
    }

    /// Consumes the iterator, returning the last element.
    ///
    /// This method evaluates the iterator until it returns [`None`], keeping
    /// track of the current element. After `None` is returned, `last()` returns
    /// the last element it saw.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().last(), Some(&3));
    ///
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().last(), Some(&5));
    ///
    /// let empty: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();
    /// assert_eq(empty.iter().last(), None);
    /// ```
    fn last(self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut last = None;
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            last = Some(x);
        }
        last
    }

    /// Returns the `n`th element of the iterator.
    ///
    /// Like most indexing operations, the count starts from zero, so `nth(0)`
    /// returns the first value, `nth(1)` the second, and so on.
    ///
    /// All preceding elements, as well as the returned element, are consumed
    /// from the iterator: calling `nth(0)` several times on the same iterator
    /// returns different elements.
    ///
    /// `nth()` returns [`None`] if `n` is greater than or equal to the length
    /// of the iterator.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().nth(1), Some(&2));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Calling `nth()` multiple times doesn't rewind the iterator:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    /// assert_eq(iter.nth(1), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.nth(1), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Returning `None` if there are fewer than `n + 1` elements:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().nth(10), None);
    /// ```
    fn nth(&mut self, n: usize) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let mut i = 0;
        while i < n {
            self.next()?;
            i += 1;
        }
        self.next()
    }

    /// Takes a closure and creates an iterator which calls that closure on each
    /// element.
    ///
    /// `map()` transforms one iterator into another, by means of its argument:
    /// a function `fn(Self::Item) -> B`. It produces a new iterator which calls
    /// this closure on each element of the original iterator.
    ///
    /// `map()` is lazy: the closure is called only as the new iterator is
    /// consumed. If you are only doing a side effect, a `for` loop or
    /// [`Self::for_each`] is clearer.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().map(|x| 2 * *x);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(4));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(6));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn map<B>(self, f: fn(Self::Item) -> B) -> Map<Self, B> {
        Map { iter: self, f }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator which uses a closure to determine if an element
    /// should be yielded.
    ///
    /// Given an element the closure must return `true` or `false`. The returned
    /// iterator will yield only the elements for which the closure returns
    /// `true`.
    ///
    /// Because the closure passed to `filter()` takes a reference, and many
    /// iterators iterate over references, this leads to a possibly confusing
    /// situation where the type of the closure's parameter is a double
    /// reference, such as `&&i32`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [0i32, 1, 2];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().filter(|x| x.is_positive());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Dereferencing the double reference:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [0, 1, 2];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().filter(|x| **x > 1);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn filter(self, pred: fn(&Self::Item) -> bool) -> Filter<Self> {
        Filter { iter: self, pred }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator which gives the current iteration count as well as
    /// the next value.
    ///
    /// The iterator returned yields pairs `(i, val)`, where `i` is the current
    /// index of iteration and `val` is the value returned by the iterator.
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// The returned iterator panics if the index of the next element would
    /// overflow a `usize`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = ['a', 'b', 'c'];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().enumerate();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some((0, &'a')));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some((1, &'b')));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some((2, &'c')));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn enumerate(self) -> Enumerate<Self> {
        Enumerate {
            iter: self,
            count: 0,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator which can use the [`Peekable::peek`] and
    /// [`Peekable::peek_mut`] methods to look at the next element of the
    /// iterator without consuming it.
    ///
    /// Note that the underlying iterator is still advanced when `peek` or
    /// `peek_mut` are called for the first time: in order to retrieve the next
    /// element, `next` is called on the underlying iterator.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let xs = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let mut iter = xs.iter().peekable();
    ///
    /// // peek() lets us see into the future
    /// assert_eq(iter.peek(), Some(&&1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    ///
    /// // we can peek() multiple times, the iterator won't advance
    /// assert_eq(iter.peek(), Some(&&3));
    /// assert_eq(iter.peek(), Some(&&3));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    ///
    /// // after the iterator is finished, so is peek()
    /// assert_eq(iter.peek(), None);
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn peekable(self) -> Peekable<Self> {
        Peekable {
            iter: self,
            peeked: None,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator that yields the first `n` elements, or fewer if the
    /// underlying iterator ends sooner.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().take(2);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// `take()` is often used with an infinite iterator, to make it finite:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let v: Vec<i32> = (0..).take(3).collect();
    /// assert_eq(v, Vec::from([0, 1, 2]));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// If fewer than `n` elements are available, `take` will limit itself to
    /// the size of the underlying iterator:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let v = Vec::from([1, 2]);
    /// let mut iter = v.into_iter().take(5);
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn take(self, n: usize) -> Take<Self> {
        Take { iter: self, n }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator that skips the first `n` elements.
    ///
    /// `skip(n)` skips elements until `n` elements are skipped or the end of
    /// the iterator is reached (whichever happens first). After that, all the
    /// remaining elements are yielded.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().skip(2);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn skip(self, n: usize) -> Skip<Self> {
        Skip { iter: self, n }
    }

    /// Folds every element into an accumulator by applying an operation,
    /// returning the final result.
    ///
    /// `fold()` takes two arguments: an initial value, and a closure with two
    /// arguments: an 'accumulator', and an element. The closure returns the
    /// value that the accumulator should have for the next iteration.
    ///
    /// The initial value is the value the accumulator will have on the first
    /// call. After applying this closure to every element of the iterator,
    /// `fold()` returns the accumulator.
    ///
    /// This operation is sometimes called 'reduce' or 'inject'. Folding is
    /// useful whenever you have a collection of something, and want to produce
    /// a single value from it. Note: `fold()`, and similar methods that
    /// traverse the entire iterator, might not terminate for infinite
    /// iterators.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// // the sum of all of the elements of the array
    /// let sum = a.iter().fold(0, |acc, x| acc + *x);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(sum, 6);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// `fold` is left-associative:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    ///
    /// let zero = "0".to_string();
    ///
    /// let result = numbers
    ///     .iter()
    ///     .fold(zero, |acc, x| `(${acc} + ${x})`.to_string());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(result, "(((((0 + 1) + 2) + 3) + 4) + 5)");
    /// ```
    fn fold<B>(self, init: B, f: fn(B, Self::Item) -> B) -> B {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut acc = init;
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            acc = f(acc, x);
        }
        acc
    }

    /// Calls a closure on each element of an iterator.
    ///
    /// This is equivalent to using a `for` loop on the iterator, although
    /// `break` and `continue` are not possible from a closure. It's generally
    /// more idiomatic to use a `for` loop, but `for_each` may be more legible
    /// when processing items at the end of longer iterator chains.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let mut v = Vec::new();
    /// (0..5).map(|x| x * 2 + 1).for_each(|x| v.push(x));
    /// assert_eq(v, Vec::from([1, 3, 5, 7, 9]));
    /// ```
    fn for_each(self, f: fn(Self::Item)) {
        let mut it = self;
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            f(x);
        }
    }

    /// Tests if any element of the iterator matches a predicate.
    ///
    /// `any()` takes a closure that returns `true` or `false`. It applies this
    /// closure to each element of the iterator, and if any of them return
    /// `true`, then so does `any()`. If they all return `false`, it returns
    /// `false`.
    ///
    /// `any()` is short-circuiting; in other words, it will stop processing as
    /// soon as it finds a `true`, given that no matter what else happens, the
    /// result will also be `true`.
    ///
    /// An empty iterator returns `false`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// assert(a.iter().any(|x| *x > 0));
    /// assert(!a.iter().any(|x| *x > 5));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Stopping at the first `true`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// assert(iter.any(|x| *x != 2));
    ///
    /// // we can still use `iter`, as there are more elements.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    /// ```
    fn any(&mut self, f: fn(Self::Item) -> bool) -> bool {
        while let Some(x) = self.next() {
            if f(x) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        false
    }

    /// Tests if every element of the iterator matches a predicate.
    ///
    /// `all()` takes a closure that returns `true` or `false`. It applies this
    /// closure to each element of the iterator, and if they all return `true`,
    /// then so does `all()`. If any of them return `false`, it returns `false`.
    ///
    /// `all()` is short-circuiting; in other words, it will stop processing as
    /// soon as it finds a `false`, given that no matter what else happens, the
    /// result will also be `false`.
    ///
    /// An empty iterator returns `true`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// assert(a.iter().all(|x| *x > 0));
    /// assert(!a.iter().all(|x| *x > 2));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Stopping at the first `false`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// assert(!iter.all(|x| *x != 2));
    ///
    /// // we can still use `iter`, as there are more elements.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    /// ```
    fn all(&mut self, f: fn(Self::Item) -> bool) -> bool {
        while let Some(x) = self.next() {
            if !f(x) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        true
    }

    /// Searches for an element of an iterator that satisfies a predicate.
    ///
    /// `find()` takes a closure that returns `true` or `false`. It applies this
    /// closure to each element of the iterator, and if any of them return
    /// `true`, then `find()` returns [`Some(element)`](Some). If they all
    /// return `false`, it returns [`None`].
    ///
    /// `find()` is short-circuiting; in other words, it will stop processing as
    /// soon as the closure returns `true`.
    ///
    /// Because `find()` takes a reference, and many iterators iterate over
    /// references, this leads to a possibly confusing situation where the
    /// argument is a double reference.
    ///
    /// If you need the index of the element, see [`Self::position`].
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().find(|x| **x == 2), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().find(|x| **x == 5), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Stopping at the first `true`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.find(|x| **x == 2), Some(&2));
    ///
    /// // we can still use `iter`, as there are more elements.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    /// ```
    fn find(&mut self, pred: fn(&Self::Item) -> bool) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        while let Some(x) = self.next() {
            if pred(&x) {
                return Some(x);
            }
        }
        None
    }

    /// Searches for an element in an iterator, returning its index.
    ///
    /// `position()` takes a closure that returns `true` or `false`. It applies
    /// this closure to each element of the iterator, and if one of them returns
    /// `true`, then `position()` returns [`Some(index)`](Some). If all of them
    /// return `false`, it returns [`None`].
    ///
    /// `position()` is short-circuiting; in other words, it will stop
    /// processing as soon as it finds a `true`. Unlike [`Self::find`], the
    /// closure takes the element itself, not a reference to it.
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// Panics if the iterator has more than `usize::MAX` non-matching
    /// elements (the index overflows).
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().position(|x| *x == 2), Some(1));
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().position(|x| *x == 5), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Stopping at the first `true`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.position(|x| *x >= 2), Some(1));
    ///
    /// // we can still use `iter`, as there are more elements.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    ///
    /// // The returned index depends on iterator state
    /// assert_eq(iter.position(|x| *x == 4), Some(0));
    /// ```
    fn position(&mut self, pred: fn(Self::Item) -> bool) -> Option<usize> {
        let mut i = 0;
        while let Some(x) = self.next() {
            if pred(x) {
                return Some(i);
            }
            i += 1;
        }
        None
    }

    /// Transforms an iterator into a collection.
    ///
    /// `collect()` can take anything iterable, and turn it into a relevant
    /// collection: any type that implements [`FromIterator`], such as [`Vec`],
    /// [`String`], `HashMap`, or an [`Option`] or [`Result`] of a collection.
    ///
    /// Because `collect()` is so general, it often needs to be told the type of
    /// the collection, with a type annotation on the variable or with the
    /// 'turbofish' `collect::<Vec<i32>>()`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let doubled: Vec<i32> = a.iter().map(|x| *x * 2).collect();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(doubled, Vec::from([2, 4, 6]));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Using the 'turbofish' instead of annotating `doubled`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let doubled = a.iter().map(|x| *x * 2).collect::<Vec<i32>>();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(doubled, Vec::from([2, 4, 6]));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Making a [`String`] from characters:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let chars = ['g', 'd', 'k', 'k', 'n'];
    ///
    /// let hello: String = chars.iter().map(|x| ((*x as u8) + 1) as char).collect();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(hello, "hello");
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Collecting an iterator of [`Result`]s stops at the first error:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let results = Vec::from([Ok(1), Err("nope"), Ok(3), Err("bad")]);
    ///
    /// let result: Result<Vec<i32>, &str> = results.into_iter().collect();
    ///
    /// // gives us the first error
    /// assert_eq(result, Err("nope"));
    ///
    /// let results: Vec<Result<i32, &str>> = Vec::from([Ok(1), Ok(3)]);
    ///
    /// let result: Result<Vec<i32>, &str> = results.into_iter().collect();
    ///
    /// // gives us the list of answers
    /// assert_eq(result, Ok(Vec::from([1, 3])));
    /// ```
    fn collect<C: FromIterator<Self::Item>>(self) -> C {
        C::from_iter(self)
    }

    /// Reverses an iterator's direction.
    ///
    /// Usually, iterators iterate from left to right. After using `rev()`, an
    /// iterator will instead iterate from right to left.
    ///
    /// This is only possible if the iterator has an end, so `rev()` only works
    /// on [`DoubleEndedIterator`]s.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().rev();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn rev(self) -> Rev<Self>
    where
        Self: DoubleEndedIterator,
    {
        Rev { iter: self }
    }

    /// 'Zips up' two iterators into a single iterator of pairs.
    ///
    /// `zip()` returns a new iterator that will iterate over two other
    /// iterators, returning a tuple where the first element comes from the
    /// first iterator, and the second element comes from the second iterator.
    ///
    /// In other words, it zips two iterators together, into a single one.
    ///
    /// If either iterator returns [`None`], `next` from the zipped iterator
    /// will return [`None`]. If the first iterator returns [`None`], `zip` will
    /// short-circuit and `next` will not be called on the second iterator.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a1 = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let a2 = [4, 5, 6];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a1.iter().zip(a2.iter());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some((&1, &4)));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some((&2, &5)));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some((&3, &6)));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Since the argument to `zip()` uses [`IntoIterator`], we can pass
    /// anything that can be converted into an [`Iterator`], not just an
    /// [`Iterator`] itself:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a1 = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let v2 = Vec::from([4, 5]);
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a1.iter().zip(v2);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some((&1, 4)));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some((&2, 5)));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// `zip()` with an infinite range works like [`Self::enumerate`]:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let enumerate: Vec<(usize, char)> = "foo".chars().enumerate().collect();
    ///
    /// let zipper: Vec<(usize, char)> = (0usize..).zip("foo".chars()).collect();
    ///
    /// assert_eq((0usize, 'f'), enumerate[0]);
    /// assert_eq((0usize, 'f'), zipper[0]);
    ///
    /// assert_eq((1usize, 'o'), enumerate[1]);
    /// assert_eq((1usize, 'o'), zipper[1]);
    ///
    /// assert_eq((2usize, 'o'), enumerate[2]);
    /// assert_eq((2usize, 'o'), zipper[2]);
    /// ```
    fn zip<U: IntoIterator>(self, other: U) -> Zip<Self, U::IntoIter> {
        Zip {
            a: self,
            b: other.into_iter(),
        }
    }

    /// Takes two iterators and creates a new iterator over both in sequence.
    ///
    /// `chain()` will return a new iterator which will first iterate over
    /// values from the first iterator and then over values from the second
    /// iterator.
    ///
    /// In other words, it links two iterators together, in a chain.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a1 = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let a2 = [4, 5, 6];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a1.iter().chain(a2.iter());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&4));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&5));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&6));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Since the argument to `chain()` uses [`IntoIterator`], we can pass
    /// anything that can be converted into an [`Iterator`] yielding the same
    /// items:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let v1 = Vec::from([1, 2]);
    /// let v: Vec<i32> = v1.into_iter().chain(Vec::from([3, 4])).collect();
    /// assert_eq(v, Vec::from([1, 2, 3, 4]));
    /// ```
    fn chain<U: IntoIterator<Item = Self::Item>>(self, other: U) -> Chain<Self, U::IntoIter> {
        Chain {
            a: self,
            b: other.into_iter(),
            first: true,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator starting at the same point, but stepping by the
    /// given amount at each iteration.
    ///
    /// The first element of the iterator is always returned, regardless of the
    /// step given; after it, every `step`th element.
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// The method will panic if the given step is `0`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().step_by(2);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&0));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&4));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn step_by(self, step: usize) -> StepBy<Self> {
        if step == 0 {
            crate::panic::panic("assertion failed: step != 0");
        }
        StepBy {
            iter: self,
            step,
            first: true,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator that both filters and maps.
    ///
    /// The returned iterator yields only the `value`s for which the supplied
    /// closure returns `Some(value)`.
    ///
    /// `filter_map` can be used to make chains of [`Self::filter`] and
    /// [`Self::map`] more concise.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = ["1", "two", "NaN", "four", "5"];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().filter_map(|s| s.parse::<i32>().ok());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(5));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn filter_map<B>(self, f: fn(Self::Item) -> Option<B>) -> FilterMap<Self, B> {
        FilterMap { iter: self, f }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator that works like map, but flattens nested structure.
    ///
    /// The [`Self::map`] adapter is very useful, but only when the closure
    /// argument produces values. If it produces an iterator (or anything that
    /// implements [`IntoIterator`]) instead, there's an extra layer of
    /// indirection. `flat_map()` will remove this extra layer on its own.
    ///
    /// You can think of `flat_map(f)` as the equivalent of mapping, and then
    /// flattening as in `map(f).flatten()`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let words = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"];
    ///
    /// let merged: String = words.iter().flat_map(|s| s.chars()).collect();
    /// assert_eq(merged, "alphabetagamma");
    /// ```
    fn flat_map<U: IntoIterator>(self, f: fn(Self::Item) -> U) -> FlatMap<Self, U> {
        FlatMap {
            iter: self,
            f,
            inner: None,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator that yields elements based on a predicate.
    ///
    /// `take_while()` takes a closure as an argument. It will call this closure
    /// on each element of the iterator, and yield elements while it returns
    /// `true`. After `false` is returned, `take_while()`'s job is over, and the
    /// rest of the elements are ignored.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-1i32, 0, 1];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().take_while(|x| x.is_negative());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&-1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Stopping after an initial `false`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-1, 0, 1, -2];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().take_while(|x| **x < 0);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&-1));
    ///
    /// // We have more elements that are less than zero, but since we already
    /// // got a false, take_while() ignores the remaining elements.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Because `take_while()` needs to look at the value in order to see if it
    /// should be included or not, consuming iterators will see that it is
    /// removed:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// let result: Vec<i32> = iter.by_ref().take_while(|n| **n != 3).cloned().collect();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(result, Vec::from([1, 2]));
    ///
    /// let result: Vec<i32> = iter.cloned().collect();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(result, Vec::from([4]));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// The `3` is no longer there, because it was consumed in order to see if
    /// the iteration should stop.
    fn take_while(self, pred: fn(&Self::Item) -> bool) -> TakeWhile<Self> {
        TakeWhile {
            iter: self,
            pred,
            done: false,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator that skips elements based on a predicate.
    ///
    /// `skip_while()` takes a closure as an argument. It will call this closure
    /// on each element of the iterator, and ignore elements until it returns
    /// `false`.
    ///
    /// After `false` is returned, `skip_while()`'s job is over, and the rest of
    /// the elements are yielded.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-1i32, 0, 1];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().skip_while(|x| x.is_negative());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&0));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// After the first `false`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-1, 0, 0, -2];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().skip_while(|x| **x < 0);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&0));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&0));
    ///
    /// // while this would have been false, since we already got a false,
    /// // skip_while() isn't used any more
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&-2));
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn skip_while(self, pred: fn(&Self::Item) -> bool) -> SkipWhile<Self> {
        SkipWhile {
            iter: self,
            pred,
            started: false,
        }
    }

    /// Does something with each element of an iterator, passing the value on.
    ///
    /// When using iterators, you'll often chain several of them together. While
    /// working on such code, you might want to check out what's happening at
    /// various parts in the pipeline. To do that, insert a call to `inspect()`.
    ///
    /// It's more common for `inspect()` to be used as a debugging tool than to
    /// exist in your final code, but applications may find it useful in certain
    /// situations when errors need to be logged before being discarded.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 4, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// // this iterator sequence is complex.
    /// let sum = a
    ///     .iter()
    ///     .cloned()
    ///     .filter(|x| *x % 2 == 0)
    ///     .fold(0, |sum, i| sum + i);
    ///
    /// println(`${sum}`);
    ///
    /// // let's add some inspect() calls to investigate what's happening
    /// let sum = a
    ///     .iter()
    ///     .cloned()
    ///     .inspect(|x| println(`about to filter: ${x}`))
    ///     .filter(|x| *x % 2 == 0)
    ///     .inspect(|x| println(`made it through filter: ${x}`))
    ///     .fold(0, |sum, i| sum + i);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(sum, 6);
    /// ```
    fn inspect(self, f: fn(&Self::Item)) -> Inspect<Self> {
        Inspect { iter: self, f }
    }

    /// Applies a function to the elements of the iterator and returns the
    /// first non-none result.
    ///
    /// `iter.find_map(f)` is equivalent to `iter.filter_map(f).next()`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = ["lol", "NaN", "2", "5"];
    ///
    /// let first_number = a.iter().find_map(|s| s.parse::<i32>().ok());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(first_number, Some(2));
    /// ```
    fn find_map<B>(&mut self, f: fn(Self::Item) -> Option<B>) -> Option<B> {
        while let Some(x) = self.next() {
            if let Some(y) = f(x) {
                return Some(y);
            }
        }
        None
    }

    /// Returns the maximum element of an iterator.
    ///
    /// If several elements are equally maximum, the last element is returned.
    /// If the iterator is empty, [`None`] is returned.
    ///
    /// The items must be [`Ord`], so this does not take floating-point numbers;
    /// use [`Self::max_by`] with `partial_cmp` or `total_cmp` for them.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let b: Vec<u32> = Vec::new();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().max(), Some(&3));
    /// assert_eq(b.iter().max(), None);
    /// ```
    fn max(self) -> Option<Self::Item>
    where
        Self::Item: Ord,
    {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut best = it.next()?;
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            if x >= best {
                best = x;
            }
        }
        Some(best)
    }

    /// Returns the minimum element of an iterator.
    ///
    /// If several elements are equally minimum, the first element is returned.
    /// If the iterator is empty, [`None`] is returned.
    ///
    /// The items must be [`Ord`], so this does not take floating-point numbers;
    /// use [`Self::min_by`] with `partial_cmp` or `total_cmp` for them.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let b: Vec<u32> = Vec::new();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().min(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(b.iter().min(), None);
    /// ```
    fn min(self) -> Option<Self::Item>
    where
        Self::Item: Ord,
    {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut best = it.next()?;
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            if x < best {
                best = x;
            }
        }
        Some(best)
    }

    /// Returns the element that gives the maximum value from the specified
    /// function.
    ///
    /// If several elements are equally maximum, the last element is returned.
    /// If the iterator is empty, [`None`] is returned.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-3i32, 0, 1, 5, -10];
    /// assert_eq(*a.iter().max_by_key(|x| x.abs()).unwrap(), -10);
    /// ```
    fn max_by_key<B: Ord>(self, f: fn(&Self::Item) -> B) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut best = it.next()?;
        let mut key = f(&best);
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            let k = f(&x);
            if k >= key {
                best = x;
                key = k;
            }
        }
        Some(best)
    }

    /// Returns the element that gives the minimum value from the specified
    /// function.
    ///
    /// If several elements are equally minimum, the first element is returned.
    /// If the iterator is empty, [`None`] is returned.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-3i32, 0, 1, 5, -10];
    /// assert_eq(*a.iter().min_by_key(|x| x.abs()).unwrap(), 0);
    /// ```
    fn min_by_key<B: Ord>(self, f: fn(&Self::Item) -> B) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut best = it.next()?;
        let mut key = f(&best);
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            let k = f(&x);
            if k < key {
                best = x;
                key = k;
            }
        }
        Some(best)
    }

    /// Returns the element that gives the maximum value with respect to the
    /// specified comparison function.
    ///
    /// If several elements are equally maximum, the last element is returned.
    /// If the iterator is empty, [`None`] is returned.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-3, 0, 1, 5, -10];
    /// assert_eq(*a.iter().max_by(|x, y| x.cmp(y)).unwrap(), 5);
    ///
    /// let f = [2.4f64, 1.3, 3.1];
    /// assert_eq(*f.iter().max_by(|x, y| x.total_cmp(y)).unwrap(), 3.1);
    /// ```
    fn max_by(
        self,
        cmp: fn(&Self::Item, &Self::Item) -> crate::cmp::Ordering,
    ) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut best = it.next()?;
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            if cmp(&x, &best) != crate::cmp::Ordering::Less {
                best = x;
            }
        }
        Some(best)
    }

    /// Returns the element that gives the minimum value with respect to the
    /// specified comparison function.
    ///
    /// If several elements are equally minimum, the first element is returned.
    /// If the iterator is empty, [`None`] is returned.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-3, 0, 1, 5, -10];
    /// assert_eq(*a.iter().min_by(|x, y| x.cmp(y)).unwrap(), -10);
    ///
    /// let f = [2.4f64, 1.3, 3.1];
    /// assert_eq(*f.iter().min_by(|x, y| x.total_cmp(y)).unwrap(), 1.3);
    /// ```
    fn min_by(
        self,
        cmp: fn(&Self::Item, &Self::Item) -> crate::cmp::Ordering,
    ) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut best = it.next()?;
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            if cmp(&x, &best) == crate::cmp::Ordering::Less {
                best = x;
            }
        }
        Some(best)
    }

    /// Sums the elements of an iterator.
    ///
    /// Takes each element, adds them together, and returns the result. An
    /// empty iterator returns the zero value of the type.
    ///
    /// `sum()` can be used to sum any type implementing [`Sum`]: the integer
    /// and floating-point types, from values (`i32: Sum<i32>`) and from
    /// references (`i32: Sum<&i32>`). The type of the result usually has to be
    /// given.
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// When summing integers, `sum()` panics if the result overflows (navi
    /// always checks integer overflow).
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let sum: i32 = a.iter().sum();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(sum, 6);
    ///
    /// let floats = [1.5, 2.5];
    /// let total: f64 = floats.iter().sum();
    /// assert_eq(total, 4.0);
    /// ```
    fn sum<S: Sum<Self::Item>>(self) -> S {
        S::sum(self)
    }

    /// Iterates over the entire iterator, multiplying all the elements.
    ///
    /// An empty iterator returns the one value of the type. It can be used for
    /// any type implementing [`Product`].
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// When multiplying integers, `product()` panics if the result overflows.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// fn factorial(n: u32) -> u32 {
    ///     (1..=n).product()
    /// }
    /// assert_eq(factorial(0), 1);
    /// assert_eq(factorial(1), 1);
    /// assert_eq(factorial(5), 120);
    /// ```
    fn product<S: Product<Self::Item>>(self) -> S {
        S::product(self)
    }

    /// Consumes an iterator, creating two collections from it.
    ///
    /// The predicate passed to `partition()` can return `true`, or `false`.
    /// `partition()` returns a pair, all of the elements for which it returned
    /// `true`, and all of the elements for which it returned `false`.
    ///
    /// The collections can be of any type that implements [`Default`] and
    /// [`Extend`], such as [`Vec`].
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = Vec::from([1, 2, 3]);
    ///
    /// let (even, odd): (Vec<i32>, Vec<i32>) = a.into_iter().partition(|n| *n % 2 == 0);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(even, Vec::from([2]));
    /// assert_eq(odd, Vec::from([1, 3]));
    /// ```
    fn partition<B>(self, pred: fn(&Self::Item) -> bool) -> (B, B)
    where
        B: Default + Extend<Self::Item>,
    {
        let mut yes = B::default();
        let mut no = B::default();
        let mut it = self;
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            if pred(&x) {
                yes.extend(Some(x));
            } else {
                no.extend(Some(x));
            }
        }
        (yes, no)
    }

    /// Creates a "by reference" adapter for this instance of [`Iterator`].
    ///
    /// Consuming method calls such as [`Self::take`] take the iterator by
    /// value, and in navi passing a value copies it: an adapter made from the
    /// iterator itself advances a copy, and the original stays where it was.
    /// `by_ref()` returns a `&mut Self`, which is itself an [`Iterator`] (there
    /// is an `impl Iterator for &mut I`), so the adapters made from it advance
    /// the original iterator.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// let first_two: Vec<i32> = iter.by_ref().take(2).cloned().collect();
    /// assert_eq(first_two, Vec::from([1, 2]));
    ///
    /// // iter went on past the two elements taken
    /// let rest: Vec<i32> = iter.cloned().collect();
    /// assert_eq(rest, Vec::from([3, 4, 5]));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Without `by_ref()`, `take` advances a copy of `iter`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// let first_two: Vec<i32> = iter.take(2).cloned().collect();
    /// assert_eq(first_two, Vec::from([1, 2]));
    ///
    /// // iter itself did not move
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    /// ```
    fn by_ref(&mut self) -> &mut Self {
        self
    }

    /// Reduces the elements to a single one, by repeatedly applying a reducing
    /// operation.
    ///
    /// If the iterator is empty, returns [`None`]; otherwise, returns the
    /// result of the reduction.
    ///
    /// The reducing function is a closure with two arguments: an 'accumulator',
    /// and an element. For iterators with at least one element, this is the
    /// same as [`Self::fold`] with the first element of the iterator as the
    /// initial accumulator value, folding every subsequent element into it.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let reduced: i32 = (1..10).reduce(|acc, e| acc + e).unwrap();
    /// assert_eq(reduced, 45);
    ///
    /// // Which is equivalent to doing it with `fold`:
    /// let folded: i32 = (1..10).fold(0, |acc, e| acc + e);
    /// assert_eq(reduced, folded);
    ///
    /// let empty: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();
    /// assert_eq(empty.into_iter().reduce(|a, b| a + b), None);
    /// ```
    fn reduce(self, f: fn(Self::Item, Self::Item) -> Self::Item) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let mut it = self;
        let first = it.next()?;
        Some(it.fold(first, f))
    }

    /// Creates an iterator that flattens nested structure.
    ///
    /// This is useful when you have an iterator of iterators or an iterator of
    /// things that can be turned into iterators and you want to remove one
    /// level of indirection.
    ///
    /// Unlike Rust's `Flatten<I>`, the returned [`Flatten`] has the item type
    /// `Self::Item` as a second type parameter.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let data = Vec::from([Vec::from([1, 2, 3, 4]), Vec::from([5, 6])]);
    /// let flattened: Vec<i32> = data.into_iter().flatten().collect();
    /// assert_eq(flattened, Vec::from([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Mapping and then flattening:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let words = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"];
    ///
    /// let merged: String = words.iter().map(|s| s.chars()).flatten().collect();
    /// assert_eq(merged, "alphabetagamma");
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Flattening works on any [`IntoIterator`] type, including [`Option`]:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let options = Vec::from([Some(123), Some(321), None, Some(231)]);
    /// let flattened: Vec<i32> = options.into_iter().flatten().collect();
    /// assert_eq(flattened, Vec::from([123, 321, 231]));
    /// ```
    fn flatten(self) -> Flatten<Self, Self::Item>
    where
        Self::Item: IntoIterator,
    {
        Flatten {
            iter: self,
            inner: None,
        }
    }

    /// Repeats an iterator endlessly.
    ///
    /// Instead of stopping at [`None`], the iterator will instead start again,
    /// from the beginning: from a clone of the original iterator, made when
    /// `cycle` is called. After iterating again, it will start at the beginning
    /// again. And again. And again. Forever. Note that in case the original
    /// iterator is empty, the resulting iterator will also be empty.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let mut it = a.iter().cycle();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(it.next(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(it.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(it.next(), Some(&3));
    /// assert_eq(it.next(), Some(&1));
    /// assert_eq(it.next(), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(it.next(), Some(&3));
    /// assert_eq(it.next(), Some(&1));
    /// ```
    fn cycle(self) -> Cycle<Self>
    where
        Self: Clone,
    {
        Cycle {
            orig: self.clone(),
            iter: self,
        }
    }

    /// An iterator adapter which, like [`Self::fold`], holds internal state,
    /// but unlike `fold`, produces a new iterator.
    ///
    /// `scan()` takes two arguments: an initial value which seeds the internal
    /// state, and a closure with two arguments, the first being a mutable
    /// reference to the internal state and the second an iterator element. The
    /// closure can assign to the internal state to share state between
    /// iterations.
    ///
    /// On iteration, the closure will be applied to each element of the
    /// iterator and the return value from the closure, an [`Option`], is
    /// returned by the `next` method. Thus the closure can return
    /// `Some(value)` to yield `value`, or `None` to end the iteration.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().scan(1, |state, x| {
    ///     // each iteration, we'll multiply the state by the element ...
    ///     *state = *state * *x;
    ///
    ///     // ... and terminate if the state exceeds 6
    ///     if *state > 6 {
    ///         None
    ///     } else {
    ///         // ... else yield the negation of the state
    ///         Some(-*state)
    ///     }
    /// });
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(-1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(-2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(-6));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn scan<St, B>(
        self,
        initial_state: St,
        f: fn(&mut St, Self::Item) -> Option<B>,
    ) -> Scan<Self, St, B> {
        Scan {
            iter: self,
            f,
            state: initial_state,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator that both yields elements based on a predicate and
    /// maps.
    ///
    /// `map_while()` takes a closure as an argument. It will call this closure
    /// on each element of the iterator, and yield elements while it returns
    /// [`Some(_)`](Some). After the first [`None`], the iterator is over (it
    /// stays over: the closure is not called again).
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-1i32, 4, 0, 1];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter().map_while(|x| 16i32.checked_div(*x));
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(-16));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(4));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Here is the same example, but with [`Self::take_while`] and
    /// [`Self::map`]:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-1i32, 4, 0, 1];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a
    ///     .iter()
    ///     .map(|x| 16i32.checked_div(*x))
    ///     .take_while(|x| x.is_some())
    ///     .map(|x| x.unwrap());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(-16));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(4));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn map_while<B>(self, predicate: fn(Self::Item) -> Option<B>) -> MapWhile<Self, B> {
        MapWhile {
            iter: self,
            f: predicate,
            done: false,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator which ends after the first [`None`].
    ///
    /// After an iterator returns [`None`], future calls may or may not yield
    /// [`Some(T)`](Some) again. `fuse()` adapts an iterator, ensuring that
    /// after a [`None`] is given, it will always return [`None`] forever.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// // an iterator which alternates between Some and None
    /// struct Alternate {
    ///     state: i32,
    /// }
    ///
    /// impl Iterator for Alternate {
    ///     type Item = i32;
    ///
    ///     fn next(&mut self) -> Option<i32> {
    ///         let val = self.state;
    ///         self.state = self.state + 1;
    ///
    ///         // if it's even, Some(i32), else None
    ///         if val % 2 == 0 { Some(val) } else { None }
    ///     }
    /// }
    ///
    /// let mut iter = Alternate { state: 0 };
    ///
    /// // we can see our iterator going back and forth
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(0));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    ///
    /// // however, once we fuse it...
    /// let mut iter = iter.fuse();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(4));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    ///
    /// // it will always return `None` after the first time.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    fn fuse(self) -> Fuse<Self> {
        Fuse {
            iter: self,
            done: false,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator which [`Clone::clone`]s all of its elements.
    ///
    /// This is useful when you have an iterator over `&T`, but you need an
    /// iterator over `T`. Unlike Rust's `Cloned<I>`, the returned [`Cloned`]
    /// has the referenced type `T` as a second type parameter.
    ///
    /// Cloning makes independent copies: cloned collections do not share their
    /// containers with the originals (see [`Self::copied`]).
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let v_cloned: Vec<i32> = a.iter().cloned().collect();
    ///
    /// // cloned is the same as .map(|x| *x), for integers
    /// let v_map: Vec<i32> = a.iter().map(|x| *x).collect();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(v_cloned, Vec::from([1, 2, 3]));
    /// assert_eq(v_map, Vec::from([1, 2, 3]));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Cloned collections are independent:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let rows = Vec::from([Vec::from([1]), Vec::from([2])]);
    ///
    /// let copies: Vec<Vec<i32>> = rows.iter().cloned().collect();
    /// let mut first = copies[0];
    /// first.push(9);
    ///
    /// // rows[0] did not change
    /// assert_eq(rows[0], Vec::from([1]));
    /// ```
    fn cloned<T: Clone>(self) -> Cloned<Self, T>
    where
        Self: Iterator<Item = &T>,
    {
        Cloned {
            iter: self,
            marker: None,
        }
    }

    /// Creates an iterator which copies all of its elements.
    ///
    /// This is useful when you have an iterator over `&T`, but you need an
    /// iterator over `T`. navi has no `Copy` trait: the copy is the shallow
    /// copy assignment makes, so copied collections share their containers
    /// with the originals; use [`Self::cloned`] for independent copies. Unlike
    /// Rust's `Copied<I>`, the returned [`Copied`] has the referenced type `T`
    /// as a second type parameter.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let v_copied: Vec<i32> = a.iter().copied().collect();
    ///
    /// // copied is the same as .map(|x| *x)
    /// let v_map: Vec<i32> = a.iter().map(|x| *x).collect();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(v_copied, Vec::from([1, 2, 3]));
    /// assert_eq(v_map, Vec::from([1, 2, 3]));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Copied collections share their containers:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let rows = Vec::from([Vec::from([1]), Vec::from([2])]);
    ///
    /// let shared: Vec<Vec<i32>> = rows.iter().copied().collect();
    /// let mut first = shared[0];
    /// first.push(9);
    ///
    /// // rows[0] is the same vector
    /// assert_eq(rows[0], Vec::from([1, 9]));
    /// ```
    fn copied<T>(self) -> Copied<Self, T>
    where
        Self: Iterator<Item = &T>,
    {
        Copied {
            iter: self,
            marker: None,
        }
    }

    /// Converts an iterator of pairs into a pair of containers.
    ///
    /// `unzip()` consumes an entire iterator of pairs, producing two
    /// collections: one from the left elements of the pairs, and one from the
    /// right elements.
    ///
    /// This function is, in some sense, the opposite of [`Self::zip`].
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [(1, 2), (3, 4), (5, 6)];
    ///
    /// let (left, right): (Vec<i32>, Vec<i32>) = a.iter().copied().unzip();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(left, Vec::from([1, 3, 5]));
    /// assert_eq(right, Vec::from([2, 4, 6]));
    /// ```
    fn unzip<A, B, FromA, FromB>(self) -> (FromA, FromB)
    where
        FromA: Default + Extend<A>,
        FromB: Default + Extend<B>,
        Self: Iterator<Item = (A, B)>,
    {
        let mut a = FromA::default();
        let mut b = FromB::default();
        let mut it = self;
        while let Some((x, y)) = it.next() {
            a.extend(Some(x));
            b.extend(Some(y));
        }
        (a, b)
    }

    /// Searches for an element in an iterator from the right, returning its
    /// index.
    ///
    /// `rposition()` takes a closure that returns `true` or `false`. It applies
    /// this closure to each element of the iterator, starting from the end,
    /// and if one of them returns `true`, then `rposition()` returns
    /// [`Some(index)`](Some). If all of them return `false`, it returns
    /// [`None`]. The index is counted from the front of the iterator.
    ///
    /// `rposition()` is short-circuiting; in other words, it will stop
    /// processing as soon as it finds a `true`.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().rposition(|x| *x == 3), Some(2));
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().rposition(|x| *x == 5), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Stopping at the first `true`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [-1, 2, 3, 4];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.rposition(|x| *x >= 2), Some(3));
    ///
    /// // we can still use `iter`, as there are more elements.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&-1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next_back(), Some(&3));
    /// ```
    fn rposition(&mut self, predicate: fn(Self::Item) -> bool) -> Option<usize>
    where
        Self: ExactSizeIterator + DoubleEndedIterator,
    {
        let mut i = self.len();
        while let Some(x) = self.next_back() {
            i -= 1;
            if predicate(x) {
                return Some(i);
            }
        }
        None
    }

    /// Checks if the elements of this iterator are sorted.
    ///
    /// That is, for each element `a` and its following element `b`, `a <= b`
    /// must hold (it returns `false` as soon as some `a > b`). If the iterator
    /// yields exactly zero or one element, `true` is returned.
    ///
    /// Unlike Rust's, a pair of elements that cannot be compared (such as a
    /// NaN and a number) does not make the result `false`: only a pair with
    /// `a > b` does.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// assert([1, 2, 2, 9].iter().is_sorted());
    /// assert(![1, 3, 2, 4].iter().is_sorted());
    /// assert([0].iter().is_sorted());
    /// assert(std::iter::empty::<i32>().is_sorted());
    /// ```
    fn is_sorted(self) -> bool
    where
        Self::Item: PartialOrd,
    {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut last = match it.next() {
            Some(x) => x,
            None => return true,
        };
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            if last > x {
                return false;
            }
            last = x;
        }
        true
    }

    /// Checks if the elements of this iterator are sorted using the given
    /// comparator function.
    ///
    /// Instead of using `PartialOrd::partial_cmp`, this function uses the given
    /// `compare` function to determine whether two elements are to be
    /// considered in sorted order: it must return `true` when its first
    /// argument may come before its second.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// assert([1, 2, 4, 9].iter().is_sorted_by(|a, b| **a <= **b));
    /// assert(![1, 2, 4, 9].iter().is_sorted_by(|a, b| **a > **b));
    ///
    /// assert([0].iter().is_sorted_by(|a, b| true));
    /// assert([0].iter().is_sorted_by(|a, b| false));
    /// assert(std::iter::empty::<i32>().is_sorted_by(|a, b| false));
    /// ```
    fn is_sorted_by(self, compare: fn(&Self::Item, &Self::Item) -> bool) -> bool {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut last = match it.next() {
            Some(x) => x,
            None => return true,
        };
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            if !compare(&last, &x) {
                return false;
            }
            last = x;
        }
        true
    }

    /// Checks if the elements of this iterator are sorted using the given key
    /// extraction function.
    ///
    /// Instead of comparing the iterator's elements directly, this function
    /// compares the keys of the elements, as determined by `f`. Apart from
    /// that, it's equivalent to [`Self::is_sorted`].
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// assert(["c", "bb", "aaa"].iter().is_sorted_by_key(|s| s.len()));
    /// assert(![-2i32, -1, 0, 3].iter().is_sorted_by_key(|n| n.abs()));
    /// ```
    fn is_sorted_by_key<K: PartialOrd>(self, f: fn(Self::Item) -> K) -> bool {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut last = match it.next() {
            Some(x) => f(x),
            None => return true,
        };
        while let Some(x) = it.next() {
            let k = f(x);
            if last > k {
                return false;
            }
            last = k;
        }
        true
    }
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for &mut I {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        (**self).next()
    }
}

/// Trait to represent types that can be created by summing up an iterator.
///
/// This trait is used to implement [`Iterator::sum`]. Types which implement
/// the trait can be generated by the `sum` method. Like [`FromIterator`],
/// this trait should rarely be called directly.
///
/// std implements it for the integer and floating-point types, both from
/// values (`i32: Sum<i32>`) and from references (`i32: Sum<&i32>`). Integer
/// sums panic on overflow.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// Implementing `Sum` for a type of your own:
///
/// ```
/// use std::iter::Sum;
///
/// #[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq)]
/// struct Meters(f64);
///
/// impl Sum for Meters {
///     fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = Meters>>(iter: I) -> Meters {
///         iter.fold(Meters(0.0), |a, b| Meters(a.0 + b.0))
///     }
/// }
///
/// let total: Meters = Vec::from([Meters(1.5), Meters(2.0)]).into_iter().sum();
/// assert_eq(total, Meters(3.5));
/// ```
pub trait Sum<A = Self> {
    /// Takes an iterator and generates `Self` from the elements by "summing up"
    /// the items.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::iter::Sum;
    ///
    /// let total: i32 = Sum::sum(Vec::from([1, 2, 3]).into_iter());
    /// assert_eq(total, 6);
    /// ```
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = A>>(iter: I) -> Self;
}

/// Trait to represent types that can be created by multiplying elements of
/// an iterator.
///
/// This trait is used to implement [`Iterator::product`]. Types which
/// implement the trait can be generated by the `product` method. Like
/// [`FromIterator`], this trait should rarely be called directly.
///
/// std implements it for the integer and floating-point types from values,
/// and for `i32`, `i64`, `u32`, `u64`, `usize`, `f32` and `f64` from
/// references too. Integer products panic on overflow.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
/// let p: i32 = a.iter().product();
/// assert_eq(p, 24);
/// ```
pub trait Product<A = Self> {
    /// Takes an iterator and generates `Self` from the elements by multiplying
    /// the items.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::iter::Product;
    ///
    /// let p: i64 = Product::product(Vec::from([2i64, 3, 7]).into_iter());
    /// assert_eq(p, 42);
    /// ```
    fn product<I: Iterator<Item = A>>(iter: I) -> Self;
}

impl Sum for i32 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = i32>>(iter: I) -> i32 {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + b)
    }
}

impl Sum for i64 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = i64>>(iter: I) -> i64 {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + b)
    }
}

impl Sum for u32 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = u32>>(iter: I) -> u32 {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + b)
    }
}

impl Sum for u64 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = u64>>(iter: I) -> u64 {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + b)
    }
}

impl Sum for usize {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = usize>>(iter: I) -> usize {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + b)
    }
}

impl Sum for f64 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = f64>>(iter: I) -> f64 {
        iter.fold(0.0, |a, b| a + b)
    }
}

impl Sum<&i32> for i32 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = &i32>>(iter: I) -> i32 {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + *b)
    }
}

impl Sum<&i64> for i64 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = &i64>>(iter: I) -> i64 {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + *b)
    }
}

impl Sum<&u32> for u32 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = &u32>>(iter: I) -> u32 {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + *b)
    }
}

impl Sum<&u64> for u64 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = &u64>>(iter: I) -> u64 {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + *b)
    }
}

impl Sum<&usize> for usize {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = &usize>>(iter: I) -> usize {
        iter.fold(0, |a, b| a + *b)
    }
}

impl Sum<&f64> for f64 {
    fn sum<I: Iterator<Item = &f64>>(iter: I) -> f64 {
        iter.fold(0.0, |a, b| a + *b)
    }
}

impl Product for i32 {
    fn product<I: Iterator<Item = i32>>(iter: I) -> i32 {
        iter.fold(1, |a, b| a * b)
    }
}

impl Product for i64 {
    fn product<I: Iterator<Item = i64>>(iter: I) -> i64 {
        iter.fold(1, |a, b| a * b)
    }
}

impl Product for u64 {
    fn product<I: Iterator<Item = u64>>(iter: I) -> u64 {
        iter.fold(1, |a, b| a * b)
    }
}

impl Product for usize {
    fn product<I: Iterator<Item = usize>>(iter: I) -> usize {
        iter.fold(1, |a, b| a * b)
    }
}

impl Product for f64 {
    fn product<I: Iterator<Item = f64>>(iter: I) -> f64 {
        iter.fold(1.0, |a, b| a * b)
    }
}

/// A double-ended iterator with the direction inverted.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::rev`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct Rev<I> {
    iter: I,
}

impl<I: DoubleEndedIterator> Iterator for Rev<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        self.iter.next_back()
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        self.iter.size_hint()
    }
}

impl<I: DoubleEndedIterator> DoubleEndedIterator for Rev<I> {
    fn next_back(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        self.iter.next()
    }
}

/// An iterator that iterates two other iterators simultaneously.
///
/// This struct is created by [`Iterator::zip`]. See its documentation for
/// more.
pub struct Zip<A, B> {
    a: A,
    b: B,
}

impl<A: Iterator, B: Iterator> Iterator for Zip<A, B> {
    type Item = (A::Item, B::Item);
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(A::Item, B::Item)> {
        let x = self.a.next()?;
        let y = self.b.next()?;
        Some((x, y))
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        let (al, ah) = self.a.size_hint();
        let (bl, bh) = self.b.size_hint();
        let hi = match (ah, bh) {
            (Some(x), Some(y)) => Some(if x < y { x } else { y }),
            (Some(x), None) => Some(x),
            (None, h) => h,
        };
        (if al < bl { al } else { bl }, hi)
    }
}

/// An iterator that links two iterators together, in a chain.
///
/// This struct is created by [`Iterator::chain`]. See its documentation for
/// more.
pub struct Chain<A, B> {
    a: A,
    b: B,
    first: bool,
}

impl<A, B> Iterator for Chain<A, B>
where
    A: Iterator,
    B: Iterator<Item = A::Item>,
{
    type Item = A::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<A::Item> {
        if self.first {
            match self.a.next() {
                Some(x) => return Some(x),
                None => {
                    self.first = false;
                }
            }
        }
        self.b.next()
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        let (al, ah) = if self.first {
            self.a.size_hint()
        } else {
            (0, Some(0))
        };
        let (bl, bh) = self.b.size_hint();
        let hi = match (ah, bh) {
            (Some(x), Some(y)) => x.checked_add(y),
            _ => None,
        };
        (al.saturating_add(bl), hi)
    }
}

/// An iterator for stepping iterators by a custom amount.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::step_by`] method. See its
/// documentation for more. Unlike Rust's, it is not double-ended.
pub struct StepBy<I> {
    iter: I,
    step: usize,
    first: bool,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for StepBy<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        if self.first {
            self.first = false;
            return self.iter.next();
        }
        self.iter.nth(self.step - 1)
    }
}

/// An iterator that uses `f` to both filter and map elements from `iter`.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::filter_map`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct FilterMap<I: Iterator, B> {
    iter: I,
    f: fn(I::Item) -> Option<B>,
}

impl<I: Iterator, B> Iterator for FilterMap<I, B> {
    type Item = B;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<B> {
        while let Some(x) = self.iter.next() {
            if let Some(y) = (self.f)(x) {
                return Some(y);
            }
        }
        None
    }
}

/// An iterator that maps each element to an iterator, and yields the
/// elements of the produced iterators.
///
/// This struct is created by [`Iterator::flat_map`]. See its documentation
/// for more. Unlike Rust's, it is not double-ended.
pub struct FlatMap<I: Iterator, U: IntoIterator> {
    iter: I,
    f: fn(I::Item) -> U,
    inner: Option<U::IntoIter>,
}

impl<I: Iterator, U: IntoIterator> Iterator for FlatMap<I, U> {
    type Item = U::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<U::Item> {
        loop {
            if let Some(inner) = &mut self.inner {
                if let Some(x) = inner.next() {
                    return Some(x);
                }
            }
            match self.iter.next() {
                Some(x) => {
                    self.inner = Some((self.f)(x).into_iter());
                }
                None => return None,
            }
        }
    }
}

/// An iterator that only accepts elements while `pred` returns `true`.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::take_while`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct TakeWhile<I: Iterator> {
    iter: I,
    pred: fn(&I::Item) -> bool,
    done: bool,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for TakeWhile<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        if self.done {
            return None;
        }
        let x = self.iter.next()?;
        if (self.pred)(&x) {
            Some(x)
        } else {
            self.done = true;
            None
        }
    }
}

/// An iterator that rejects elements while `pred` returns `true`.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::skip_while`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct SkipWhile<I: Iterator> {
    iter: I,
    pred: fn(&I::Item) -> bool,
    started: bool,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for SkipWhile<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        loop {
            let x = self.iter.next()?;
            if self.started || !(self.pred)(&x) {
                self.started = true;
                return Some(x);
            }
        }
    }
}

/// An iterator that calls a function with a reference to each element
/// before yielding it.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::inspect`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct Inspect<I: Iterator> {
    iter: I,
    f: fn(&I::Item),
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Inspect<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        let x = self.iter.next()?;
        (self.f)(&x);
        Some(x)
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        self.iter.size_hint()
    }
}

/// An iterator that yields an element exactly once.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`once`] function. See its documentation
/// for more.
pub struct Once<T> {
    v: Option<T>,
}

/// Creates an iterator that yields an element exactly once.
///
/// This is commonly used to adapt a single value into a [`Iterator::chain`]
/// of other kinds of iteration. Maybe you have an iterator that covers
/// almost everything, but you need an extra special case. Maybe you have a
/// function which works on iterators, but you only need to process one
/// value.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// Basic usage:
///
/// ```
/// use std::iter;
///
/// // one is the loneliest number
/// let mut one = iter::once(1);
///
/// assert_eq(one.next(), Some(1));
///
/// // just one, that's all we get
/// assert_eq(one.next(), None);
/// ```
///
/// Chaining together with another iterator:
///
/// ```
/// use std::iter;
///
/// let v: Vec<i32> = iter::once(0).chain(Vec::from([1, 2, 3])).collect();
/// assert_eq(v, Vec::from([0, 1, 2, 3]));
/// ```
pub fn once<T>(v: T) -> Once<T> {
    Once { v: Some(v) }
}

impl<T> Iterator for Once<T> {
    type Item = T;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        self.v.take()
    }
}

/// An iterator that repeats an element endlessly.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`repeat`] function. See its documentation
/// for more.
pub struct Repeat<T: Clone> {
    v: T,
}

/// Creates a new iterator that endlessly repeats a single element.
///
/// The `repeat()` function repeats a single value over and over again: each
/// element is a [`Clone::clone`] of `v`, so repeating a collection yields
/// independent collections.
///
/// Infinite iterators like `repeat()` are often used with adapters like
/// [`Iterator::take`], in order to make them finite.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// Basic usage:
///
/// ```
/// use std::iter;
///
/// // the number four 4ever:
/// let mut fours = iter::repeat(4);
///
/// assert_eq(Some(4), fours.next());
/// assert_eq(Some(4), fours.next());
/// assert_eq(Some(4), fours.next());
/// assert_eq(Some(4), fours.next());
///
/// // yup, still four
/// assert_eq(Some(4), fours.next());
/// ```
///
/// Going finite with [`Iterator::take`]:
///
/// ```
/// use std::iter;
///
/// // that last example was too many fours. Let's only have four fours.
/// let four_fours: Vec<i32> = iter::repeat(4).take(4).collect();
///
/// assert_eq(four_fours, Vec::from([4, 4, 4, 4]));
/// ```
pub fn repeat<T: Clone>(v: T) -> Repeat<T> {
    Repeat { v }
}

impl<T: Clone> Iterator for Repeat<T> {
    type Item = T;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        Some(self.v.clone())
    }
}

/// An iterator that yields nothing.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`empty`] function. See its documentation
/// for more.
pub struct Empty<T> {
    marker: Option<T>,
}

/// Creates an iterator that yields nothing.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// use std::iter;
///
/// // this could have been an iterator over i32, but alas, it's just not.
/// let mut nope = iter::empty::<i32>();
///
/// assert_eq(None, nope.next());
/// ```
pub fn empty<T>() -> Empty<T> {
    Empty { marker: None }
}

impl<T> Iterator for Empty<T> {
    type Item = T;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        None
    }
}

/// Conversion into an [`Iterator`].
///
/// By implementing `IntoIterator` for a type, you define how it will be
/// converted to an iterator. This is common for types which describe a
/// collection of some kind.
///
/// One benefit of implementing `IntoIterator` is that your type will work
/// with `for` loops: `for x in v` calls `v.into_iter()`.
///
/// Every [`Iterator`] implements `IntoIterator`, returning itself. navi has
/// no moves, so `into_iter` does not use up a collection: [`Vec`]'s, for
/// example, iterates over the vector's shared buffer, and the vector stays
/// usable.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// Basic usage:
///
/// ```
/// let v = Vec::from([1, 2, 3]);
/// let mut iter = v.into_iter();
///
/// assert_eq(Some(1), iter.next());
/// assert_eq(Some(2), iter.next());
/// assert_eq(Some(3), iter.next());
/// assert_eq(None, iter.next());
/// ```
///
/// Implementing `IntoIterator` for your type:
///
/// ```
/// // A sample collection, that's just a wrapper over Vec<i32>
/// struct MyCollection {
///     items: Vec<i32>,
/// }
///
/// impl MyCollection {
///     fn new() -> MyCollection {
///         MyCollection { items: Vec::new() }
///     }
///
///     fn add(&mut self, elem: i32) {
///         self.items.push(elem);
///     }
/// }
///
/// // and we'll implement IntoIterator
/// impl IntoIterator for MyCollection {
///     type Item = i32;
///     type IntoIter = std::vec::IntoIter<i32>;
///
///     fn into_iter(self) -> std::vec::IntoIter<i32> {
///         self.items.into_iter()
///     }
/// }
///
/// // Now we can make a new collection...
/// let mut c = MyCollection::new();
///
/// // ... add some stuff to it ...
/// c.add(0);
/// c.add(1);
/// c.add(2);
///
/// // ... and then turn it into an Iterator:
/// let mut n = 0;
/// for i in c {
///     assert_eq(i, n);
///     n += 1;
/// }
/// assert_eq(n, 3);
/// ```
///
/// It is common to use `IntoIterator` as a trait bound, so that a function
/// takes both collections and iterators:
///
/// ```
/// fn sum_all<T: IntoIterator<Item = i32>>(values: T) -> i32 {
///     let mut total = 0;
///     for v in values {
///         total += v;
///     }
///     total
/// }
///
/// assert_eq(sum_all(Vec::from([1, 2, 3])), 6);
/// assert_eq(sum_all(1..4), 6);
/// ```
pub trait IntoIterator {
    /// The type of the elements being iterated over.
    type Item;
    /// Which kind of iterator are we turning this into?
    type IntoIter: Iterator<Item = Self::Item>;
    /// Creates an iterator from a value.
    ///
    /// See the [`IntoIterator`] documentation for more.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let v = Vec::from([1, 2, 3]);
    /// let mut iter = v.into_iter();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(Some(1), iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(Some(2), iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(Some(3), iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(None, iter.next());
    /// ```
    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter;
}

impl<I: Iterator> IntoIterator for I {
    type Item = I::Item;
    type IntoIter = I;
    fn into_iter(self) -> I {
        self
    }
}

/// Conversion from an [`Iterator`].
///
/// By implementing `FromIterator` for a type, you define how it will be
/// created from an iterator. This is common for types which describe a
/// collection of some kind.
///
/// If you want to create a collection from the contents of an iterator,
/// the [`Iterator::collect`] method is preferred. However, when you need to
/// specify the container type, [`FromIterator::from_iter`] can be more
/// readable than using a turbofish (e.g. `::<Vec<_>>()`).
///
/// std implements it for the collections ([`Vec`], [`String`] from
/// characters and strings, the maps and sets), and for [`Option`] and
/// [`Result`] of a collection (stopping at the first [`None`] or error).
///
/// # Examples
///
/// Basic usage:
///
/// ```
/// let five_fives = std::iter::repeat(5).take(5);
///
/// let v = Vec::from_iter(five_fives);
///
/// assert_eq(v, Vec::from([5, 5, 5, 5, 5]));
/// ```
///
/// Using [`Iterator::collect`] to implicitly use `FromIterator`:
///
/// ```
/// let five_fives = std::iter::repeat(5).take(5);
///
/// let v: Vec<i32> = five_fives.collect();
///
/// assert_eq(v, Vec::from([5, 5, 5, 5, 5]));
/// ```
///
/// Implementing `FromIterator` for your type:
///
/// ```
/// // A sample collection, that's just a wrapper over Vec<i32>
/// #[derive(Debug)]
/// struct MyCollection {
///     items: Vec<i32>,
/// }
///
/// impl MyCollection {
///     fn new() -> MyCollection {
///         MyCollection { items: Vec::new() }
///     }
///
///     fn add(&mut self, elem: i32) {
///         self.items.push(elem);
///     }
/// }
///
/// // and we'll implement FromIterator
/// impl FromIterator<i32> for MyCollection {
///     fn from_iter<I: IntoIterator<Item = i32>>(iter: I) -> MyCollection {
///         let mut c = MyCollection::new();
///
///         for i in iter {
///             c.add(i);
///         }
///
///         c
///     }
/// }
///
/// // Now we can make a new iterator...
/// let iter = (0..5).into_iter();
///
/// // ... and make a MyCollection out of it
/// let c = MyCollection::from_iter(iter);
///
/// assert_eq(c.items, Vec::from([0, 1, 2, 3, 4]));
///
/// // collect works too!
///
/// let iter = (0..5).into_iter();
/// let c: MyCollection = iter.collect();
///
/// assert_eq(c.items, Vec::from([0, 1, 2, 3, 4]));
/// ```
pub trait FromIterator<A> {
    /// Creates a value from an iterator.
    ///
    /// See the [`FromIterator`] documentation for more.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let five_fives = std::iter::repeat(5).take(5);
    ///
    /// let v = Vec::from_iter(five_fives);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(v, Vec::from([5, 5, 5, 5, 5]));
    /// ```
    fn from_iter<T: IntoIterator<Item = A>>(iter: T) -> Self;
}

/// Extend a collection with the contents of an iterator.
///
/// Iterators produce a series of values, and collections can also be
/// thought of as a series of values. The `Extend` trait bridges this gap,
/// allowing you to extend a collection by including the contents of that
/// iterator. When extending a collection with an already existing key, that
/// entry is updated or, in the case of collections that permit multiple
/// entries with equal keys, that entry is inserted.
///
/// A pair of collections extends from an iterator of pairs: `(EA, EB)`
/// implements `Extend<(A, B)>` when `EA: Extend<A>` and `EB: Extend<B>`
/// (as [`Iterator::unzip`] does).
///
/// # Examples
///
/// Basic usage:
///
/// ```
/// // You can extend a String with some chars:
/// let mut message = "The first three letters are: ".to_string();
///
/// message.extend(['a', 'b', 'c'].iter());
///
/// assert_eq("abc", &message[29..32]);
/// ```
///
/// Implementing `Extend`:
///
/// ```
/// // A sample collection, that's just a wrapper over Vec<i32>
/// #[derive(Debug)]
/// struct MyCollection {
///     items: Vec<i32>,
/// }
///
/// impl MyCollection {
///     fn new() -> MyCollection {
///         MyCollection { items: Vec::new() }
///     }
///
///     fn add(&mut self, elem: i32) {
///         self.items.push(elem);
///     }
/// }
///
/// // since MyCollection has a list of i32s, we implement Extend for i32
/// impl Extend<i32> for MyCollection {
///     // This is a bit simpler with the concrete type signature: we can call
///     // extend on anything which can be turned into an Iterator which gives
///     // us i32s. Because we need i32s to put into MyCollection.
///     fn extend<T: IntoIterator<Item = i32>>(&mut self, iter: T) {
///         // The implementation is very straightforward: loop through the
///         // iterator, and add() each element to ourselves.
///         for elem in iter {
///             self.add(elem);
///         }
///     }
/// }
///
/// let mut c = MyCollection::new();
///
/// c.add(5);
/// c.add(6);
/// c.add(7);
///
/// // let's extend our collection with three more numbers
/// c.extend(Vec::from([1, 2, 3]));
///
/// assert_eq(c.items, Vec::from([5, 6, 7, 1, 2, 3]));
/// ```
///
/// Extending a pair of vectors:
///
/// ```
/// let mut pair: (Vec<i32>, Vec<char>) = (Vec::new(), Vec::new());
/// pair.extend(Vec::from([(1, 'a'), (2, 'b')]));
/// assert_eq(pair.0, Vec::from([1, 2]));
/// assert_eq(pair.1, Vec::from(['a', 'b']));
/// ```
pub trait Extend<A> {
    /// Extends a collection with the contents of an iterator.
    ///
    /// As this is the only required method for this trait, the [`Extend`] docs
    /// contain more details.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// // You can extend a String with some chars:
    /// let mut message = "abc".to_string();
    ///
    /// message.extend(['d', 'e', 'f'].iter());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(message, "abcdef");
    /// ```
    fn extend<T: IntoIterator<Item = A>>(&mut self, iter: T);
}

/// An iterator able to yield elements from both ends.
///
/// Something that implements `DoubleEndedIterator` has one extra capability
/// over something that implements [`Iterator`]: the ability to also take
/// `Item`s from the back, as well as the front.
///
/// It is important to note that both back and forth work on the same range,
/// and do not cross: iteration is over when they meet in the middle.
///
/// In a similar fashion to the [`Iterator`] protocol, once a
/// `DoubleEndedIterator` returns [`None`] from a [`Self::next_back`],
/// calling it again may or may not ever return [`Some`] again.
/// [`Iterator::next`] and [`Self::next_back`] are interchangeable for this
/// purpose.
///
/// Unlike Rust's, [`Flatten`], [`FlatMap`] and [`StepBy`] do not implement
/// this trait.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// Basic usage:
///
/// ```
/// let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
///
/// let mut iter = numbers.iter();
///
/// assert_eq(Some(&1), iter.next());
/// assert_eq(Some(&6), iter.next_back());
/// assert_eq(Some(&5), iter.next_back());
/// assert_eq(Some(&2), iter.next());
/// assert_eq(Some(&3), iter.next());
/// assert_eq(Some(&4), iter.next());
/// assert_eq(None, iter.next());
/// assert_eq(None, iter.next_back());
/// ```
pub trait DoubleEndedIterator: Iterator {
    /// Removes and returns an element from the end of the iterator.
    ///
    /// Returns [`None`] when there are no more elements.
    ///
    /// The [trait-level](DoubleEndedIterator) docs contain more details.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = numbers.iter();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(Some(&1), iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(Some(&6), iter.next_back());
    /// assert_eq(Some(&5), iter.next_back());
    /// assert_eq(Some(&2), iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(Some(&3), iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(Some(&4), iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(None, iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(None, iter.next_back());
    /// ```
    fn next_back(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;

    /// Returns the `n`th element from the end of the iterator.
    ///
    /// This is essentially the reversed version of [`Iterator::nth`]. Although
    /// like most indexing operations, the count starts from zero, so
    /// `nth_back(0)` returns the first value from the end, `nth_back(1)` the
    /// second, and so on.
    ///
    /// All elements between the end and the returned element, as well as the
    /// returned element itself, are consumed from the iterator: calling
    /// `nth_back(0)` several times on the same iterator returns different
    /// elements.
    ///
    /// `nth_back()` returns [`None`] if `n` is greater than or equal to the
    /// length of the iterator.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().nth_back(2), Some(&1));
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Calling `nth_back()` multiple times doesn't rewind the iterator:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.nth_back(1), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(iter.nth_back(1), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Returning `None` if there are fewer than `n + 1` elements:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().nth_back(10), None);
    /// ```
    fn nth_back(&mut self, n: usize) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        let mut i = 0;
        while i < n {
            self.next_back()?;
            i += 1;
        }
        self.next_back()
    }

    /// An iterator method that reduces the iterator's elements to a single,
    /// final value, starting from the back.
    ///
    /// This is the reverse version of [`Iterator::fold`]: it takes elements
    /// starting from the back of the iterator.
    ///
    /// `rfold()` takes two arguments: an initial value, and a closure with two
    /// arguments: an 'accumulator', and an element. The closure returns the
    /// value that the accumulator should have for the next iteration.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// // the sum of all of the elements of a
    /// let sum = a.iter().rfold(0, |acc, x| acc + *x);
    ///
    /// assert_eq(sum, 6);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// This example demonstrates the right-associative nature of `rfold()`: it
    /// builds a string, starting with an initial value and continuing with
    /// each element from the back until the front:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    ///
    /// let zero = "0".to_string();
    ///
    /// let result = numbers
    ///     .iter()
    ///     .rfold(zero, |acc, x| `(${x} + ${acc})`.to_string());
    ///
    /// assert_eq(result, "(1 + (2 + (3 + (4 + (5 + 0)))))");
    /// ```
    fn rfold<B>(self, init: B, f: fn(B, Self::Item) -> B) -> B {
        let mut it = self;
        let mut acc = init;
        while let Some(x) = it.next_back() {
            acc = f(acc, x);
        }
        acc
    }

    /// Searches for an element of an iterator from the back that satisfies a
    /// predicate.
    ///
    /// `rfind()` takes a closure that returns `true` or `false`. It applies
    /// this closure to each element of the iterator, starting at the end, and
    /// if any of them return `true`, then `rfind()` returns
    /// [`Some(element)`](Some). If they all return `false`, it returns
    /// [`None`].
    ///
    /// `rfind()` is short-circuiting; in other words, it will stop processing
    /// as soon as the closure returns `true`.
    ///
    /// Because `rfind()` takes a reference, and many iterators iterate over
    /// references, this leads to a possibly confusing situation where the
    /// argument is a double reference.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().rfind(|x| **x == 2), Some(&2));
    /// assert_eq(a.iter().rfind(|x| **x == 5), None);
    /// ```
    ///
    /// Stopping at the first `true`:
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.rfind(|x| **x == 2), Some(&2));
    ///
    /// // we can still use `iter`, as there are more elements.
    /// assert_eq(iter.next_back(), Some(&1));
    /// ```
    fn rfind(&mut self, predicate: fn(&Self::Item) -> bool) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        while let Some(x) = self.next_back() {
            if predicate(&x) {
                return Some(x);
            }
        }
        None
    }
}

/// An iterator that knows its exact length.
///
/// Many [`Iterator`]s don't know how many times they will iterate, but some
/// do. If an iterator knows how many times it can iterate, providing access
/// to that information can be useful. For example, if you want to iterate
/// backwards, a good start is to know where the end is.
///
/// When implementing an `ExactSizeIterator`, you must also implement
/// [`Iterator`]. The default implementation of [`Self::len`] returns the
/// lower bound of [`Iterator::size_hint`], so either the iterator's
/// `size_hint` must be exact, or `len` must be overridden (as the integer
/// ranges do: their `size_hint` is the default `(0, None)`).
///
/// The slice and vector iterators, the integer ranges (`Range` of all the
/// integer types but the 128-bit ones, `RangeInclusive` of `u8`, `u16`,
/// `i8` and `i16`) and the adapters keeping the length implement it.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// Basic usage:
///
/// ```
/// // a finite range knows exactly how many times it will iterate
/// let five = 0..5;
///
/// assert_eq(five.len(), 5);
/// ```
///
/// Implementing it for an iterator of your own:
///
/// ```
/// // An iterator which counts from one to five.
/// struct Counter {
///     count: usize,
/// }
///
/// impl Iterator for Counter {
///     type Item = usize;
///
///     fn next(&mut self) -> Option<usize> {
///         if self.count < 5 {
///             self.count += 1;
///             Some(self.count)
///         } else {
///             None
///         }
///     }
///
///     // the exact number of the elements left
///     fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
///         (5 - self.count, Some(5 - self.count))
///     }
/// }
///
/// // the default len() uses size_hint()
/// impl ExactSizeIterator for Counter {}
///
/// let mut counter = Counter { count: 0 };
///
/// assert_eq(counter.len(), 5);
/// let _ = counter.next();
/// assert_eq(counter.len(), 4);
/// ```
pub trait ExactSizeIterator: Iterator {
    /// Returns the exact remaining length of the iterator.
    ///
    /// The default implementation returns the lower bound of
    /// [`Iterator::size_hint`], which must then be exact.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = a.iter();
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.len(), 5);
    /// assert_eq(Some(&1), iter.next());
    /// assert_eq(iter.len(), 4);
    /// ```
    fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.size_hint().0
    }
}

/// An iterator that maps the values of `iter` with `f`.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::map`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct Map<I: Iterator, B> {
    iter: I,
    f: fn(I::Item) -> B,
}

impl<I: Iterator, B> Iterator for Map<I, B> {
    type Item = B;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<B> {
        match self.iter.next() {
            Some(x) => Some((self.f)(x)),
            None => None,
        }
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        self.iter.size_hint()
    }
}

/// An iterator that filters the elements of `iter` with `pred`.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::filter`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct Filter<I: Iterator> {
    iter: I,
    pred: fn(&I::Item) -> bool,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Filter<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        while let Some(x) = self.iter.next() {
            if (self.pred)(&x) {
                return Some(x);
            }
        }
        None
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        (0, self.iter.size_hint().1)
    }
}

/// An iterator that yields the current count and the element during
/// iteration.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::enumerate`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct Enumerate<I> {
    iter: I,
    count: usize,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Enumerate<I> {
    type Item = (usize, I::Item);
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(usize, I::Item)> {
        let x = self.iter.next()?;
        let i = self.count;
        self.count += 1;
        Some((i, x))
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        self.iter.size_hint()
    }
}

/// An iterator that only iterates over the first `n` iterations of `iter`.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::take`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct Take<I> {
    iter: I,
    n: usize,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Take<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        if self.n == 0 {
            return None;
        }
        self.n -= 1;
        self.iter.next()
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        if self.n == 0 {
            return (0, Some(0));
        }
        let (lo, hi) = self.iter.size_hint();
        let lo = if lo < self.n { lo } else { self.n };
        let hi = match hi {
            Some(h) => {
                if h < self.n {
                    h
                } else {
                    self.n
                }
            }
            None => self.n,
        };
        (lo, Some(hi))
    }
}

/// An iterator that skips over `n` elements of `iter`.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::skip`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct Skip<I> {
    iter: I,
    n: usize,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Skip<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        while self.n > 0 {
            self.n -= 1;
            self.iter.next()?;
        }
        self.iter.next()
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        let (lo, hi) = self.iter.size_hint();
        (
            lo.saturating_sub(self.n),
            match hi {
                Some(h) => Some(h.saturating_sub(self.n)),
                None => None,
            },
        )
    }
}

/// An iterator with a `peek()` that returns an optional reference to the
/// next element.
///
/// This struct is created by the [`Iterator::peekable`] method. See its
/// documentation for more.
pub struct Peekable<I: Iterator> {
    iter: I,
    peeked: Option<Option<I::Item>>,
}

impl<I: Iterator> Peekable<I> {
    /// Returns a reference to the [`Iterator::next`] value without advancing
    /// the iterator.
    ///
    /// Like `next`, if there is a value, it is wrapped in a `Some(T)`. But if
    /// the iteration is over, `None` is returned.
    ///
    /// Because `peek()` returns a reference, and many iterators iterate over
    /// references, there can be a possibly confusing situation where the
    /// return value is a double reference.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// let xs = [1, 2, 3];
    ///
    /// let mut iter = xs.iter().peekable();
    ///
    /// // peek() lets us see into the future
    /// assert_eq(iter.peek(), Some(&&1));
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    ///
    /// // The iterator does not advance even if we `peek` multiple times
    /// assert_eq(iter.peek(), Some(&&3));
    /// assert_eq(iter.peek(), Some(&&3));
    ///
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    ///
    /// // After the iterator is finished, so is `peek()`
    /// assert_eq(iter.peek(), None);
    /// assert_eq(iter.next(), None);
    /// ```
    pub fn peek(&mut self) -> Option<&I::Item> {
        if self.peeked.is_none() {
            self.peeked = Some(self.iter.next());
        }
        match &self.peeked {
            Some(Some(v)) => Some(v),
            _ => None,
        }
    }
}

impl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Peekable<I> {
    type Item = I::Item;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {
        match self.peeked.take() {
            Some(v) => v,
            None => self.iter.next(),
        }
    }
}

/// Types with successor and predecessor operations, used by range
/// iteration (`a..b`, `a..=b`, `a..`). Implemented by the compiler for the
/// integer types and `char`.
///
/// Rust's `Step` is unstable; navi's has only the two methods below.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// use std::iter::Step;
///
/// assert_eq(Step::forward(1u8, 2), 3u8);
/// assert_eq(Step::backward('c', 2), 'a');
/// ```
pub trait Step: Clone + PartialOrd {
    /// Returns the value `n` steps after `start`.
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// Panics if the result would overflow the type.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::iter::Step;
    ///
    /// assert_eq(Step::forward(10i32, 5), 15);
    /// ```
    fn forward(start: Self, n: usize) -> Self;
    /// Returns the value `n` steps before `start`.
    ///
    /// # Panics
    ///
    /// Panics if the result would overflow the type.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::iter::Step;
    ///
    /// assert_eq(Step::backward(10i32, 5), 5);
    /// ```
    fn backward(start: Self, n: usize) -> Self;
}

// (start < end: the successor of start and the predecessor of end exist.)
impl<A> Iterator for crate::ops::Range<A>
where
    A: Step + StepChecked,
{
    type Item = A;

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        A::steps_between(&self.start, &self.end)
    }

    // Large only before its comparison and clone become operations.
    #[inline(always)]
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<A> {
        if self.start < self.end {
            let n = self.start.clone();
            self.start = n.checked_succ().unwrap();
            Some(n)
        } else {
            None
        }
    }
}

impl<A> DoubleEndedIterator for crate::ops::Range<A>
where
    A: Step + StepChecked,
{
    fn next_back(&mut self) -> Option<A> {
        if self.start < self.end {
            self.end = self.end.checked_pred().unwrap();
            Some(self.end.clone())
        } else {
            None
        }
    }
}

/// The next and previous values of the [`Step`] types, [`None`] at their
/// ends (so that an inclusive range up to the type's maximum ends without
/// overflowing).
///
/// Implemented for the integer types and `char`; used by the iterators
/// of `a..b` and `a..=b`.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// use std::iter::StepChecked;
///
/// assert_eq(5u8.checked_succ(), Some(6));
/// assert_eq(255u8.checked_succ(), None);
/// assert_eq(0u8.checked_pred(), None);
/// ```
pub trait StepChecked {
    /// Returns the next value, or [`None`] if `self` is the type's maximum.
    ///
    /// For `char`, the next value after `'\u{D7FF}'` is `'\u{E000}'` (the
    /// surrogates are not `char`s), and `char::MAX` has none.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::iter::StepChecked;
    ///
    /// assert_eq(41i32.checked_succ(), Some(42));
    /// assert_eq(i32::MAX.checked_succ(), None);
    /// assert_eq('a'.checked_succ(), Some('b'));
    /// assert_eq('\u{D7FF}'.checked_succ(), Some('\u{E000}'));
    /// ```
    fn checked_succ(&self) -> Option<Self>;
    /// Returns the previous value, or [`None`] if `self` is the type's minimum.
    ///
    /// For `char`, the value before `'\u{E000}'` is `'\u{D7FF}'`, and `'\0'`
    /// has none.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::iter::StepChecked;
    ///
    /// assert_eq(43i32.checked_pred(), Some(42));
    /// assert_eq(i32::MIN.checked_pred(), None);
    /// assert_eq('\u{E000}'.checked_pred(), Some('\u{D7FF}'));
    /// assert_eq('\0'.checked_pred(), None);
    /// ```
    fn checked_pred(&self) -> Option<Self>;
    /// How many successor steps lead from `start` to `end` (as Rust's
    /// `Step::steps_between`): the lower bound and, if it fits in a `usize`,
    /// the upper; `(0, Some(0))` when `end` is not after `start`. `(0, None)`
    /// unless the type says.
    ///
    /// # Examples
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::iter::StepChecked;
    ///
    /// assert_eq(i32::steps_between(&-2, &3), (5, Some(5)));
    /// assert_eq(u8::steps_between(&9, &1), (0, Some(0)));
    /// assert_eq(char::steps_between(&'\u{D7FF}', &'\u{E000}'), (1, Some(1)));
    /// ```
    fn steps_between(_start: &Self, _end: &Self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        (0, None)
    }
}

// steps_between from the difference of two values (as u128; 0 if not after).
fn steps_of(d: u128) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
    if d <= usize::MAX as u128 {
        (d as usize, Some(d as usize))
    } else {
        (usize::MAX, None)
    }
}

impl StepChecked for u8 {
    // No calls: inlined into the range loops.
    #[inline]
    fn checked_succ(&self) -> Option<u8> {
        if *self == 255u8 {
            None
        } else {
            Some(*self + 1)
        }
    }

    #[inline]
    fn checked_pred(&self) -> Option<u8> {
        if *self == 0u8 { None } else { Some(*self - 1) }
    }

    fn steps_between(start: &u8, end: &u8) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        if *end <= *start {
            return (0, Some(0));
        }
        steps_of((*end - *start) as u128)
    }
}

impl StepChecked for u16 {
    // No calls: inlined into the range loops.
    #[inline]
    fn checked_succ(&self) -> Option<u16> {
        if *self == 65535u16 {
            None
        } else {
            Some(*self + 1)
        }
    }

    #[inline]
    fn checked_pred(&self) -> Option<u16> {
        if *self == 0u16 { None } else { Some(*self - 1) }
    }

    fn steps_between(start: &u16, end: &u16) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        if *end <= *start {
            return (0, Some(0));
        }
        steps_of((*end - *start) as u128)
